    }
    List<Map.Entry<String,Integer>> list = new ArrayList<>(counts.entrySet());
    Collections.sort(list, (a, b) -> b.getValue() - a.getValue());
    for (int i = 0; i < 10 && i < list.size(); i++)
        System.out.println(list.get(i).getKey() + " " + list.get(i).getValue());
}
```

Here's a cleaned-up version. The main fixes are closing the file, splitting on any whitespace, a simpler counting idiom, and a sort that can't overflow.

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.Locale;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public final class WordCount {
    private static final Pattern WHITESPACE = Pattern.compile("\\s+");

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        if (args.length != 1) {
            System.err.println("usage: WordCount <file>");
            System.exit(2);
        }

        Map<String, Long> counts;
        try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(Path.of(args[0]), StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {
            counts = lines
                    .flatMap(WHITESPACE::splitAsStream)
                    .filter(w -> !w.isEmpty())
                    .map(w -> w.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT))
                    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Function.identity(), Collectors.counting()));
        }

        counts.entrySet().stream()
                .sorted(Map.Entry.<String, Long>comparingByValue().reversed()
                        .thenComparing(Map.Entry.comparingByKey()))
                .limit(10)
                .forEach(e -> System.out.println(e.getKey() + " " + e.getValue()));
    }
}
```

What changed:

- **Resource leak:** the original never closed the reader. `Files.lines` returns a stream backed by an open file, so it goes in try-with-resources, which closes it even if an exception is thrown.
- **Splitting:** `split(" ")` breaks on single spaces only, so tabs and runs of spaces produced odd tokens. `\s+` handles any whitespace. Leading whitespace still yields one empty token, hence the `filter`.
- **Counting:** `groupingBy` with `counting()` replaces the `containsKey`/`get`/`put` dance. If you prefer the loop style, `counts.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum)` is the one-line equivalent.
- **Comparator:** `b.getValue() - a.getValue()` is a classic bug pattern; subtraction can overflow for large values and flip the order. `comparingByValue().reversed()` is correct and readable. The added `thenComparing` on the key makes ties come out alphabetically instead of in hash order, so output is deterministic.
- **`Locale.ROOT`:** plain `toLowerCase()` uses the default locale, and in a Turkish locale `"TITLE"` lowercases to a dotless ı. `Locale.ROOT` gives consistent results everywhere.
- **Explicit UTF-8:** `FileReader` without a charset used the platform default before Java 18. `Files.lines` defaults to UTF-8 anyway, but stating it documents intent.

`throws IOException` instead of `throws Exception` keeps the signature honest about what can actually fail. Note that punctuation is still part of words ("end." vs "end"); if that matters, split on `[^\p{L}\p{N}']+` instead of whitespace.

A `sealed interface` is exactly the tool for this. All implementations must be declared in the same package and module, so the compiler knows the complete set and can check that a `when` covers every case.

```kotlin
sealed interface ApiResult<out T> {
    data class Success<T>(val data: T) : ApiResult<T>
    data class HttpError(val code: Int, val body: String?) : ApiResult<Nothing>
    data class NetworkError(val cause: Throwable) : ApiResult<Nothing>
}
```

The `out T` variance plus `Nothing` for the error cases means an `HttpError` is an `ApiResult<User>`, an `ApiResult<List<Order>>`, or anything else, without needing a type parameter of its own.

Producing results, for example in a repository:

```kotlin
suspend fun fetchUser(id: String): ApiResult<User> =
    try {
        val response = client.get("/users/$id")
        if (response.isSuccessful) {
            ApiResult.Success(response.parse<User>())
        } else {
            ApiResult.HttpError(response.code, response.bodyAsText())
        }
    } catch (e: IOException) {
        ApiResult.NetworkError(e)
    }
```

Only `IOException` is caught, so programming errors (a `NullPointerException`, a parsing bug) still crash loudly instead of being disguised as "no connection". In coroutine code this also matters because catching a broad `Exception` would swallow `CancellationException` and break cancellation.

Consuming them:

```kotlin
val message = when (val result = fetchUser("42")) {
    is ApiResult.Success      -> "Hello, ${result.data.name}"
    is ApiResult.HttpError    -> if (result.code == 404) "No such user" else "Server error ${result.code}"
    is ApiResult.NetworkError -> "Check your connection"
}
```

Because `when` is used as an expression, it must be exhaustive. If you later add a `data class Timeout(...)` case, every such `when` stops compiling until it's handled, which is precisely the guarantee you asked for. Smart casts give you `result.data` and `result.code` without manual casting.

Avoid adding an `else ->` branch to these `when`s, since it silently absorbs new cases and defeats the check.

A couple of helpers make common call sites shorter:

```kotlin
inline fun <T, R> ApiResult<T>.map(transform: (T) -> R): ApiResult<R> = when (this) {
    is ApiResult.Success      -> ApiResult.Success(transform(data))
    is ApiResult.HttpError    -> this
    is ApiResult.NetworkError -> this
}

fun <T> ApiResult<T>.getOrNull(): T? = (this as? ApiResult.Success)?.data
```

Kotlin's built-in `Result<T>` only distinguishes success from a `Throwable`, so it can't express "HTTP error with code" as a first-class case without wrapping it in an exception. That's why a custom sealed type fits this situation better.

A good modern approach combines three ideas: a grid-stride loop so each thread accumulates many elements, warp shuffles for the in-warp reduction, and one `atomicAdd` per block to combine block results.

```cuda
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__inline__ __device__ float warp_reduce_sum(float v)
{
    for (int offset = warpSize / 2; offset > 0; offset /= 2)
        v += __shfl_down_sync(0xffffffffu, v, offset);
    return v;
}

__global__ void sum_kernel(const float* __restrict__ in, size_t n, float* out)
{
    float acc = 0.0f;
    size_t stride = (size_t)blockDim.x * gridDim.x;
    for (size_t i = (size_t)blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; i < n; i += stride)
        acc += in[i];

    acc = warp_reduce_sum(acc);

    __shared__ float warp_sums[32];               // one slot per warp
    int lane = threadIdx.x % warpSize;
    int warp = threadIdx.x / warpSize;
    if (lane == 0)
        warp_sums[warp] = acc;
    __syncthreads();

    if (warp == 0) {
        int num_warps = (blockDim.x + warpSize - 1) / warpSize;
        acc = (lane < num_warps) ? warp_sums[lane] : 0.0f;
        acc = warp_reduce_sum(acc);
        if (lane == 0)
            atomicAdd(out, acc);
    }
}

void device_sum(const float* d_in, size_t n, float* d_out, cudaStream_t stream)
{
    int device, sms;
    cudaGetDevice(&device);
    cudaDeviceGetAttribute(&sms, cudaDevAttrMultiProcessorCount, device);

    const int threads = 256;
    const int blocks  = sms * 8;                   // enough to fill the GPU

    cudaMemsetAsync(d_out, 0, sizeof(float), stream);
    sum_kernel<<<blocks, threads, 0, stream>>>(d_in, n, d_out);
}
```

Why it's built this way:

- **Grid-stride loop with a fixed grid size.** Launching one thread per element for hundreds of millions of elements creates huge numbers of blocks that each do almost nothing. A few blocks per SM that loop over the data keep loads coalesced and make the reduction overhead negligible.
- **`__shfl_down_sync`** exchanges registers within a warp without shared memory or `__syncthreads`. The full mask is valid because every thread of the block reaches both calls.
- **Shared memory holds just one value per warp**, and a single `__syncthreads()` is needed. The block size must be a multiple of 32 and at most 1024 for `warp_sums[32]` to suffice.
- **One atomic per block** means only a few hundred atomics in total, which costs nothing compared with reading the array.

Numerical caveat: float atomics complete in nondeterministic order, so results can differ in the last bits between runs, and summing hundreds of millions of floats in single precision loses accuracy. If that matters, accumulate in `double` per thread, or write per-block partial sums to an array and reduce them in a second, fixed-order pass for bitwise-reproducible results.

In production, check the return codes of the CUDA API calls and use `cudaGetLastError()` after the launch. Libraries like CUB (`cub::DeviceReduce::Sum`) implement a heavily tuned version of this, so prefer them unless you need to fuse the reduction with other work.

This 1D blur kernel hangs or gives wrong values at the end of the array. n isn't a multiple of the block size. RADIUS is 3, block size 256.

```cuda
__global__ void blur(const float* in, float* out, int n) {
    __shared__ float tile[256 + 2 * RADIUS];
    int g = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    int l = threadIdx.x + RADIUS;
    if (g < n) {
        tile[l] = in[g];
        if (threadIdx.x < RADIUS) {
            tile[l - RADIUS] = in[g - RADIUS];
            tile[l + blockDim.x] = in[g + blockDim.x];
        }
        __syncthreads();
        float s = 0;
        for (int k = -RADIUS; k <= RADIUS; k++) s += tile[l + k];
        out[g] = s / (2 * RADIUS + 1);
    }
}
```

There are three separate bugs here.

**1. `__syncthreads()` inside a divergent branch.** In the last block, threads with `g >= n` skip the `if` body and never reach the barrier. `__syncthreads()` must be reached by every thread in the block (or by none); otherwise the behavior is undefined, which shows up as hangs or as threads reading the tile before it's filled.

**2. Out-of-bounds halo loads.** In block 0, `in[g - RADIUS]` reads `in[-3]` through `in[-1]`. In the last block, `in[g + blockDim.x]` can be past `n`. Both are invalid global memory accesses.

**3. Uninitialized tile slots in the last block.** When the last block is partial, the threads near the end of the valid range read `tile` entries for positions just past `n - 1`. Those slots would have been filled by the threads with `g >= n`, but those threads skipped the load, so the sums for the last few elements include garbage from shared memory.

The robust pattern is: every thread participates in loading, out-of-range reads are replaced with a defined boundary value, all threads hit the barrier, and only the store is guarded.

```cuda
#define RADIUS 3
#define BLOCK  256

__device__ __forceinline__ float load_clamped(const float* in, int i, int n)
{
    i = max(0, min(i, n - 1));                 // clamp-to-edge boundary
    return in[i];
}

__global__ void blur(const float* __restrict__ in, float* __restrict__ out, int n)
{
    __shared__ float tile[BLOCK + 2 * RADIUS];

    int base = blockIdx.x * BLOCK;             // first element of this block
    // Cooperative load of BLOCK + 2*RADIUS elements, halo included.
    for (int t = threadIdx.x; t < BLOCK + 2 * RADIUS; t += BLOCK)
        tile[t] = load_clamped(in, base + t - RADIUS, n);

    __syncthreads();                           // reached by every thread

    int g = base + threadIdx.x;
    if (g < n) {
        int l = threadIdx.x + RADIUS;
        float s = 0.0f;
        for (int k = -RADIUS; k <= RADIUS; ++k)
            s += tile[l + k];
        out[g] = s / (2 * RADIUS + 1);
    }
}
```

Launch it with exactly `BLOCK` threads per block:

```cuda
blur<<<(n + BLOCK - 1) / BLOCK, BLOCK>>>(d_in, d_out, n);
```

Notes on the fix:

- **The strided loading loop** fills the whole tile, halo included, without special-casing which threads load the halo. Each tile index `t` maps to global index `base + t - RADIUS`, so the right halo of a partial last block is correct automatically.
- **Clamp-to-edge** is one boundary policy. If your blur should treat outside values as zero, return `0.0f` for out-of-range indices instead. Either way the policy is explicit rather than reading garbage.
- **The kernel requires `blockDim.x == BLOCK`**, since the shared array is sized at compile time. Add `assert(blockDim.x == BLOCK)` in debug builds if other launch sites exist.
- **`n` must be at least 1** for the clamp to be valid; guard the launch on the host.

`compute-sanitizer --tool memcheck` reports the original out-of-bounds reads, and `--tool synccheck` flags the divergent barrier.

